/// Name of the fork. A fork is additional data associated with a filesystem object.
///
/// On Linux, a resource fork is used to store additional data with a filesystem object. A file
/// always has at least one fork for the data portion, and additional forks may exist.
/// On NTFS, this is analogous to an Alternate Data Stream (ADS), and the default data stream for
/// a file is just called $DATA. Zone.Identifier is commonly used by Windows to track contents
/// downloaded from the internet. An ADS is typically of the form:
/// C:\path\to\filename.extension:some_fork_name, and some_fork_name is the value that should
/// populate `fork_name`. `filename.extension` should populate `file.name`, and `extension`
/// should populate `file.extension`. The full path, `file.path`, will include the fork name.
